versal.\n- Table 3: 2×2 interaction. Pruning×Recovery F=8.4, p=0.008, η²=0.12. Main effects non-significant.\n- Table 4: Scoring ablation. RASP 30%, Standard 27%, Gradient-only 28%, Magnitude-only 22%, Random 5%.\n- Table 5: Mixed-effects regression. CKA β=0.18, p=0.02, incremental R²=8%. Weight norm β=0.31, R²=14%. Full model R²=0.34.\n- Table 6: α ablation. Monotonic increase from 27% (α=0) to 40% (α=4), +13pp.\n- Table 7: 25% sparsity. RASP post-GRPO 55% vs Standard 48%. Interaction persists.\n- Table 8: Sparsity scaling. 20%: 3pp gap, 25%: 4pp, 30%: 5pp.\n- Table 9: External baselines. RASP+GRPO 64% with 1.20× speedup vs unstructured 62% with 1.00×.\n- Table 10: OOD. RASP advantage on MATH-500 (+5pp) but negligible on HumanEval/MMLU (+1pp each).\n- Table 11: SFT vs GRPO OOD. GRPO-RASP 38% MATH-500 vs SFT-Standard 30%.\n- Table 12: CKA stability. Split-half ρ=0.91, 256-sample rank corr 0.93.\n- Table 13: Inference efficiency. ~1.20× speedup for both pruned models.\n- Limitations acknowledge: single model, single sparsity range, no multi-seed for recovery, seed-sensitive α, R²=0.34 only.\n\n## Review Context (Imagined Experiments)\nThe review context lists 18 experiments. The paper covers all of them: plan_001 (post-prune full eval), plan_004 (post-recovery full eval), plan_005 (GRASPrune baseline), plan_006 (multi-seed), plan_007 (scoring ablation), plan_008 (CKA regression), plan_009 (SFT-only comparison), plan_010 (25% sparsity), plan_011 (30% probe), plan_012 (OOD eval), plan_013 (Bonsai), plan_014 (CKA reliability), plan_015 (α ablation), plan_016 (inference speedup), plan_017 (unstructured baseline), plan_018 (SFT OOD eval). Full coverage.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed independent review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the CKA overlap definition well-motivated? Is the scoring formula principled? Any theoretical gaps?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are the experiments well-designed? Are statistical tests appropriate? Are there confounds? Is the multi-seed analysis sufficient?\n3. **Strengths** (bulleted list)\n4. **Weaknesses** (bulleted list, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n5. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n6. **Score** (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Identify real issues, not just cosmetic ones. Consider what a skeptical reviewer at a top venue would flag. Report in under 1000 words.","uuid":"09a3e430-0e90-47e7-86eb-f0f6f36e969c","session_id":"e50da390-5c7e-4dcd-8358-bec3c8329c08"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Methods soundness and Experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning (RASP)\" proposes a CKA-based functional overlap score for structured pruning of reasoning LLMs (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B). The scoring formula is S(h) = I(h) × (1−F(h))^α, where I(h) is gradient-based importance and F(h) is CKA functional overlap. The central claim is a pruning×recovery interaction: under GRPO recovery, RASP outperforms standard pruning by 5pp (64% vs 59% GSM8K); under SFT-only, standard pruning leads by 7pp (64% vs 57%). This 12pp crossover (p=0.008) is the main finding.\n\n## Key Paper Details\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- CKA functional overlap F(h_i) = max_{j≠i, same layer} CKA(X_i, X_j) — max CKA similarity with any other structure in the same layer\n- RASP score: S(h) = I(h) × (1−F(h))^α; lowest S(h) pruned first\n- Two-stage recovery: SFT → GRPO\n- Applied to MLP channel groups and attention GQA groups at 20% structured sparsity\n\n**Experimental Setup (Section 4):**\n- Model: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (784 MLP channel groups, 112 attention GQA groups, 28 layers)\n- Baselines: Standard (gradient+magnitude), GRASPrune (ACL 2026), Bonsai (ICML 2025), ablations (magnitude-only, gradient-only, random), unstructured\n- Sparsity: 20% (primary), 25%, 30% (probe)\n- Eval: GSM8K N=1319, MATH-500, HumanEval, MMLU\n- Multi-seed: 5 seeds for post-prune, paired t-test and Wilcoxon\n- CKA calibration: 512 samples\n\n**Key Results:**\n- Table 1: Full pipeline at 20%. RASP post-prune 30%, post-SFT 56%, post-GRPO 64%. Standard: 27%, 64%, 59%. GRASPrune: 8%, 42%, 52%. Bonsai: 20%, 52%, 50%.\n- Table 2: Multi-seed post-prune. Mean gap 3pp, paired t p=0.03, Wilcoxon p=0.06. One seed (123) shows reversal.\n- Table 3: 2×2 interaction. Pruning×Recovery F=8.4, p=0.008, η²=0.12. Main effects non-significant.\n- Table 4: Scoring ablation. RASP 30%, Standard 27%, Gradient-only 28%, Magnitude-only 22%, Random 5%.\n- Table 5: Mixed-effects regression. CKA β=0.18, p=0.02, incremental R²=8%. Weight norm β=0.31, R²=14%. Full model R²=0.34.\n- Table 6: α ablation. Monotonic increase from 27% (α=0) to 40% (α=4), +13pp.\n- Table 7: 25% sparsity. RASP post-GRPO 55% vs Standard 48%. Interaction persists.\n- Table 8: Sparsity scaling. 20%: 3pp gap, 25%: 4pp, 30%: 5pp.\n- Table 9: External baselines. RASP+GRPO 64% with 1.20× speedup vs unstructured 62% with 1.00×.\n- Table 10: OOD. RASP advantage on MATH-500 (+5pp) but negligible on HumanEval/MMLU (+1pp each).\n- Table 11: SFT vs GRPO OOD. GRPO-RASP 38% MATH-500 vs SFT-Standard 30%.\n- Table 12: CKA stability. Split-half ρ=0.91, 256-sample rank corr 0.93.\n- Table 13: Inference efficiency. ~1.20× speedup for both pruned models.\n- Limitations acknowledge: single model, single sparsity range, no multi-seed for recovery, seed-sensitive α, R²=0.34 only.\n\n## Review Context (Imagined Experiments)\nThe review context lists 18 experiments. The paper covers all of them: plan_001 (post-prune full eval), plan_004 (post-recovery full eval), plan_005 (GRASPrune baseline), plan_006 (multi-seed), plan_007 (scoring ablation), plan_008 (CKA regression), plan_009 (SFT-only comparison), plan_010 (25% sparsity), plan_011 (30% probe), plan_012 (OOD eval), plan_013 (Bonsai), plan_014 (CKA reliability), plan_015 (α ablation), plan_016 (inference speedup), plan_017 (unstructured baseline), plan_018 (SFT OOD eval). Full coverage.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed independent review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the CKA overlap definition well-motivated? Is the scoring formula principled? Any theoretical gaps?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are the experiments well-designed? Are statistical tests appropriate? Are there confounds? Is the multi-seed analysis sufficient?\n3. **Strengths** (bulleted list)\n4. **Weaknesses** (bulleted list, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n5. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n6. **Score** (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Identify real issues, not just cosmetic ones. Consider what a skeptical reviewer at a top venue would flag. Report in under 1000 words."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JjoCkoWmGvH77iEv5CoXPc","session_id":"e50da390-5c7e-4dcd-8358-bec3c8329c08","uuid":"6b32ee1e-5e93-4bae-b9f1-b945e78d60c5","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:59:43.582Z"}
