{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JjoCkoWmGvH77iEv5CoXPc","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nThe review identifies five categories of issues:\n\n**Critical findings:**\n1. The paper's entire evidential foundation -- all post-GRPO numbers (RASP 70%, Standard 53%, 17pp gap, 25pp interaction p=0.0008) -- is imagined. GRPO v1-v7 all failed; v8 is still running. The only completed recovery experiments (SFT) show Standard > RASP, the opposite of the claimed narrative.\n2. The 2x2 interaction ANOVA has N=1 per cell with zero residual degrees of freedom, making the reported p-values statistically invalid.\n\n**Major findings:**\n3. Multi-seed results (5 seeds, paired t p=0.0002) are entirely imagined. Known calibration sensitivity (D022) contradicts claimed stability.\n4. The alpha ablation actual data is non-monotonic (30%, 25%, 27%, 29%, 40%) but reported as clean monotonic (25%, 27%, 29%, 31%, 35%), with a calibration confound between actual and reported conditions.\n5. No theoretical justification for the multiplicative scoring formula.\n\n**Moderate findings:**\n6. Near-circularity in Claim 2 (CKA overlap predicting recoverability of removed structures is close to tautological).\n7. CKA computed on mean-pooled activations collapses temporal structure critical for reasoning.\n8. Single model, narrow sparsity range (20-25%).\n\nThe review is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_17/projects/recovery_aware_struct_prune/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T140651/best/review_report.md`. The key message: the research question is excellent, but the paper cannot be evaluated on projected numbers. If GRPO v8 delivers significant results, the score could rise to Weak Accept contingent on fixing the statistical methodology."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a0b78c672e863caf8 (use SendMessage with to: 'a0b78c672e863caf8' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 78889\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 136846</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e50da390-5c7e-4dcd-8358-bec3c8329c08","uuid":"8d78fc54-6af7-4bf0-956a-45f7080949be","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:02:00.429Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Methods soundness and Experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning (RASP)\" proposes a CKA-based functional overlap score for structured pruning of reasoning LLMs (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B). The scoring formula is S(h) = I(h) × (1−F(h))^α, where I(h) is gradient-based importance and F(h) is CKA functional overlap. The central claim is a pruning×recovery interaction: under GRPO recovery, RASP outperforms standard pruning by 5pp (64% vs 59% GSM8K); under SFT-only, standard pruning leads by 7pp (64% vs 57%). This 12pp crossover (p=0.008) is the main finding.\n\n## Key Paper Details\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- CKA functional overlap F(h_i) = max_{j≠i, same layer} CKA(X_i, X_j) — max CKA similarity with any other structure in the same layer\n- RASP score: S(h) = I(h) × (1−F(h))^α; lowest S(h) pruned first\n- Two-stage recovery: SFT → GRPO\n- Applied to MLP channel groups and attention GQA groups at 20% structured sparsity\n\n**Experimental Setup (Section 4):**\n- Model: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (784 MLP channel groups, 112 attention GQA groups, 28 layers)\n- Baselines: Standard (gradient+magnitude), GRASPrune (ACL 2026), Bonsai (ICML 2025), ablations (magnitude-only, gradient-only, random), unstructured\n- Sparsity: 20% (primary), 25%, 30% (probe)\n- Eval: GSM8K N=1319, MATH-500, HumanEval, MMLU\n- Multi-seed: 5 seeds for post-prune, paired t-test and Wilcoxon\n- CKA calibration: 512 samples\n\n**Key Results:**\n- Table 1: Full pipeline at 20%. RASP post-prune 30%, post-SFT 56%, post-GRPO 64%. Standard: 27%, 64%, 59%. GRASPrune: 8%, 42%, 52%. Bonsai: 20%, 52%, 50%.\n- Table 2: Multi-seed post-prune. Mean gap 3pp, paired t p=0.03, Wilcoxon p=0.06. One seed (123) shows reversal.\n- Table 3: 2×2 interaction. Pruning×Recovery F=8.4, p=0.008, η²=0.12. Main effects non-significant.\n- Table 4: Scoring ablation. RASP 30%, Standard 27%, Gradient-only 28%, Magnitude-only 22%, Random 5%.\n- Table 5: Mixed-effects regression. CKA β=0.18, p=0.02, incremental R²=8%. Weight norm β=0.31, R²=14%. Full model R²=0.34.\n- Table 6: α ablation. Monotonic increase from 27% (α=0) to 40% (α=4), +13pp.\n- Table 7: 25% sparsity. RASP post-GRPO 55% vs Standard 48%. Interaction persists.\n- Table 8: Sparsity scaling. 20%: 3pp gap, 25%: 4pp, 30%: 5pp.\n- Table 9: External baselines. RASP+GRPO 64% with 1.20× speedup vs unstructured 62% with 1.00×.\n- Table 10: OOD. RASP advantage on MATH-500 (+5pp) but negligible on HumanEval/MMLU (+1pp each).\n- Table 11: SFT vs GRPO OOD. GRPO-RASP 38% MATH-500 vs SFT-Standard 30%.\n- Table 12: CKA stability. Split-half ρ=0.91, 256-sample rank corr 0.93.\n- Table 13: Inference efficiency. ~1.20× speedup for both pruned models.\n- Limitations acknowledge: single model, single sparsity range, no multi-seed for recovery, seed-sensitive α, R²=0.34 only.\n\n## Review Context (Imagined Experiments)\nThe review context lists 18 experiments. The paper covers all of them: plan_001 (post-prune full eval), plan_004 (post-recovery full eval), plan_005 (GRASPrune baseline), plan_006 (multi-seed), plan_007 (scoring ablation), plan_008 (CKA regression), plan_009 (SFT-only comparison), plan_010 (25% sparsity), plan_011 (30% probe), plan_012 (OOD eval), plan_013 (Bonsai), plan_014 (CKA reliability), plan_015 (α ablation), plan_016 (inference speedup), plan_017 (unstructured baseline), plan_018 (SFT OOD eval). Full coverage.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed independent review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the CKA overlap definition well-motivated? Is the scoring formula principled? Any theoretical gaps?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are the experiments well-designed? Are statistical tests appropriate? Are there confounds? Is the multi-seed analysis sufficient?\n3. **Strengths** (bulleted list)\n4. **Weaknesses** (bulleted list, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n5. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n6. **Score** (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Identify real issues, not just cosmetic ones. Consider what a skeptical reviewer at a top venue would flag. Report in under 1000 words.","agentId":"a0b78c672e863caf8","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nThe review identifies five categories of issues:\n\n**Critical findings:**\n1. The paper's entire evidential foundation -- all post-GRPO numbers (RASP 70%, Standard 53%, 17pp gap, 25pp interaction p=0.0008) -- is imagined. GRPO v1-v7 all failed; v8 is still running. The only completed recovery experiments (SFT) show Standard > RASP, the opposite of the claimed narrative.\n2. The 2x2 interaction ANOVA has N=1 per cell with zero residual degrees of freedom, making the reported p-values statistically invalid.\n\n**Major findings:**\n3. Multi-seed results (5 seeds, paired t p=0.0002) are entirely imagined. Known calibration sensitivity (D022) contradicts claimed stability.\n4. The alpha ablation actual data is non-monotonic (30%, 25%, 27%, 29%, 40%) but reported as clean monotonic (25%, 27%, 29%, 31%, 35%), with a calibration confound between actual and reported conditions.\n5. No theoretical justification for the multiplicative scoring formula.\n\n**Moderate findings:**\n6. Near-circularity in Claim 2 (CKA overlap predicting recoverability of removed structures is close to tautological).\n7. CKA computed on mean-pooled activations collapses temporal structure critical for reasoning.\n8. Single model, narrow sparsity range (20-25%).\n\nThe review is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_17/projects/recovery_aware_struct_prune/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T140651/best/review_report.md`. The key message: the research question is excellent, but the paper cannot be evaluated on projected numbers. If GRPO v8 delivers significant results, the score could rise to Weak Accept contingent on fixing the statistical methodology."}],"totalDurationMs":136846,"totalTokens":78889,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3516,"cache_read_input_tokens":74886,"output_tokens":486,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3516},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":486,"cache_read_input_tokens":74886,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3516,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3516,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"acd669781ea567186","tool_use_id":"toolu_01XRXoNWibjzgLu2t7Vuw955","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":74193,"tool_uses":18,"duration_ms":134039},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"378c5cba-27d2-4aa0-b344-ebfde6b22f7e","session_id":"e50da390-5c7e-4dcd-8358-bec3c8329c08"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CSQ9BYB32vLbpW7jG8zhBo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EbgrbQCnaYur7FjCkVvqzg","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"recovery_aware_struct_prune","tier":"median","dream_id":"dream_20260515T140651","overall_score":"3 - Weak Reject","report":"# Reviewer R2: Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\n\n## Summary\n\nThe paper introduces RASP, a CKA-based functional overlap scoring method for structured pruning of reasoning LLMs. The central finding is a pruning x recovery interaction: RASP outperforms standard pruning under GRPO recovery (+5pp) while standard pruning outperforms RASP under SFT-only recovery (+7pp), yielding a 12pp crossover (p=0.008). CKA functional overlap provides incremental R^2=8% as a recoverability predictor.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n**The interaction finding is the paper's strongest novelty claim, but it is narrow.** The observation that different recovery paradigms prefer different pruning structures is genuinely interesting and, to my knowledge, not explicitly studied before. However, this is an empirical observation on a single model at a single sparsity level, without a convincing mechanistic explanation beyond post-hoc appeal to \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands.\"\n\n**The CKA-based scoring is incremental, not novel.** MPruner (2024) already uses CKA for pruning decisions. The paper correctly notes the granularity difference (intra-layer vs. inter-layer, width vs. depth), but the core idea -- use CKA similarity to identify redundant structures for removal -- is directly inherited. The formula S(h) = I(h) x (1-F(h))^alpha is a straightforward multiplicative combination of importance and overlap; there is no learned component, no theoretical justification for the functional form, and no comparison against other functional forms (e.g., additive: I(h) + beta*(1-F(h)), or using mutual information instead of CKA).\n\n**The \"Redundancy Paradox\" framing overstates novelty.** The idea that redundant structures can be safely pruned because their function is distributed elsewhere is the standard justification for redundancy-based pruning since at least Redundancy-Aware Pruning (2020). Reframing this as a \"paradox\" is rhetorically effective but conceptually the same insight. The true novelty is the recovery-paradigm dependence, not the redundancy concept itself.\n\n## 2. Related Work Coverage\n\n**Several important works are missing:**\n\n- **FANG (Dec 2025)**: Function-Aware Neuron Grouping for structured pruning. This is the closest conceptual parallel -- both identify functionally critical neurons, FANG via activation-based grouping, RASP via CKA. Not citing this is a significant oversight.\n- **FuncNet (Aug 2025)**: ICA-based functional network identification for LLM pruning. Same motivation (treat LLM as having functional subnetworks), different technique.\n- **Davari et al. (ICLR 2023)**: CKA reliability limitations. Any paper building on CKA must acknowledge that CKA is sensitive to outliers and can be manipulated without changing functional behavior. The paper uses CKA uncritically.\n- **Boiling Frog (Feb 2026)**: Iterative prune-tune loop for reasoning model compression. Directly relevant as a prune-then-recover approach.\n- **From LLMs to LRMs (Jan 2026)**: Width vs. depth pruning for reasoning models. Validates the width-pruning focus but also provides important context about why reasoning models need specialized pruning.\n- **PAT (AAAI 2025)**: Joint pruning-tuning framework. Directly addresses the pruning-recovery coupling that RASP claims to study.\n- **RestoreLCC (NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight)**: Alternative recovery via activation compensation. Relevant recovery baseline.\n- **P2 Law (ACL 2025)**: Scaling laws for pruning recovery. Relevant theoretical context.\n\nThe related work section is too thin for a top venue -- 10 references with only 4 paragraphs. The positioning against MPruner is fair but brief. The \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\" connection is used extensively in the narrative but the mechanistic link to pruning structure selection is hand-wavy.\n\n## 3. Contribution Significance\n\n**Claim 1 (CKA-aware pruning advantage under GRPO): Weak.** The 5pp advantage (64% vs 59%) is modest. The paired t-test p=0.03 at the post-prune stage is based on 5 seeds and is marginal under Wilcoxon (p=0.06). Critically, multi-seed validation covers only the post-prune stage, not the recovery pipeline. A single GRPO run per method means the 5pp GRPO gap has unknown variance from the recovery stage itself.\n\n**Claim 2 (CKA predictive signal): Modest.** Incremental R^2=8% is real but unimpressive. Weight norm (14%) and gradient importance (11%) are individually stronger. CKA is the weakest significant predictor among four. Calling this a \"complementary signal\" is honest, but it undermines the paper's pitch that CKA captures something fundamentally different.\n\n**Claim 3 (Interaction effect): The strongest claim, but fragile.** The 12pp interaction (p=0.008, eta^2=0.12) is statistically significant with a medium effect size. However: (a) it rests on a single GRPO run per condition -- no multi-seed recovery -- so the denominator in the ANOVA is unclear; (b) the 2x2 design has only 4 cells, making the F-test sensitive to any single outlier; (c) eta^2=0.12 is conventionally \"medium\" but not large. For a paper whose entire narrative rests on this interaction, the evidence base is thin.\n\n**Collectively, the contributions are below the bar for a top venue.** A 5pp advantage on one model at one sparsity, an 8% incremental R^2 that is weaker than simpler baselines, and a 12pp interaction from an unreplicated 2x2 design do not constitute a compelling package.\n\n## 4. Strengths\n\n- The pruning x recovery interaction is a genuinely novel research question. Framing pruning and recovery as coupled rather than independent stages is a useful conceptual contribution.\n- The 2x2 factorial design (pruning method x recovery method) is methodologically sound in principle and provides clean causal evidence for the interaction.\n- Honest reporting of the SFT reversal (Standard > RASP) rather than cherry-picking only GRPO results.\n- The alpha ablation is informative: monotonic improvement from alpha=0 to alpha=4 confirms CKA's directional value.\n- CKA reliability analysis (split-half rho=0.91, calibration size sensitivity) addresses practical concerns.\n- The paper correctly scopes Claim 2 to MLP groups and acknowledges the 0/112 attention pruning limitation.\n- Inference speedup measurement (1.20x) confirms practical relevance of structured pruning.\n\n## 5. Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) No multi-seed recovery runs.** The central claims about GRPO recovery (5pp advantage, 12pp interaction) rest on a single GRPO training run per condition. GRPO has high variance -- a single run could easily shift by 5pp. The ANOVA p=0.008 is computed using what degrees of freedom? If this is a bootstrap over the test set, it does not account for training variance.\n\n- **(Major) Single model.** All experiments use DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B. No validation on Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-8B, or any other architecture. For a claim about the fundamental coupling of pruning and recovery, single-model evidence is insufficient.\n\n- **(Major) Missing critical related work.** FANG, FuncNet, Davari et al. CKA reliability, Boiling Frog, PAT, and RestoreLCC are all highly relevant and missing. This weakens the positioning claim that \"no prior work studies pruning-recovery interaction\" -- PAT explicitly co-optimizes pruning and tuning.\n\n- **(Moderate) CKA is the weakest predictor in its own regression.** Weight norm and gradient importance both have higher beta and higher incremental R^2 than CKA. The paper acknowledges this but then builds its entire method around CKA. Why not build S(h) around weight norm, which has 14% incremental R^2 vs CKA's 8%?\n\n- **(Moderate) alpha=1 default is suboptimal by the paper's own data.** The alpha ablation shows monotonic improvement up to alpha=4 (40% vs 27%), yet the main results use alpha=1 (30%). This 10pp gap between the default and the apparent optimum is larger than the claimed RASP advantage over Standard. The paper's \"conservative default\" argument is unsatisfying.\n\n- **(Moderate) Only MLP groups pruned at 20-25%.** 0/112 attention GQA groups are pruned under any method. The CKA signal is validated exclusively on MLP channel groups. The generality claim -- that CKA captures \"functional uniqueness\" of model structures -- is tested on only one structure type.\n\n- **(Moderate) OOD advantage is domain-limited.** RASP advantage is +5pp on GSM8K and MATH-500 (both math) but only +1pp on HumanEval and MMLU. The method appears math-reasoning-specific rather than generally applicable to reasoning LLMs.\n\n- **(Minor) The \"Redundancy Paradox\" is not actually paradoxical.** Redundant structures being safe to remove is the standard intuition, not a paradox. The paper's framing that \"redundancy is a resource for RLVR\" is a restatement of \"removing redundant things is safer than removing unique things.\"\n\n- **(Minor) Inconsistency in F(h) definition.** The method section defines F(h) as max CKA with any same-layer structure (Eq. 4), but the paper summary describes it as mean pairwise CKA. Which was actually used?\n\n## 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. What are the degrees of freedom in the 2x2 ANOVA (Table 3)? If each cell has a single training run, the interaction F-test has no denominator df from training variance. Is the p=0.008 derived from bootstrap over the test set only?\n\n2. Have you run GRPO recovery with more than one random seed per pruning method? If not, how do you rule out that the 5pp gap is within GRPO's training variance?\n\n3. Why is alpha=1 the default when alpha=2 and alpha=4 are strictly better in your own ablation? Did you validate post-GRPO accuracy for alpha=4, or only post-prune?\n\n4. CKA's incremental R^2 (8%) is lower than weight norm (14%) and gradient importance (11%). Would S(h) = I(h) x (1-WeightNormOverlap)^alpha outperform the CKA-based version? Have you tried?\n\n5. The post-prune eval bug documented in your project history (D021-D025) -- where Standard's accuracy was originally 1% but actually ~27% -- raises concerns about data integrity throughout. Can you confirm that all numbers in the current paper have been re-validated with the corrected evaluation pipeline?\n\n6. FANG (Dec 2025) groups neurons by functional roles for structured pruning. How does RASP's CKA-based overlap differ from FANG's activation-based grouping in practice? Have you compared against FANG as a baseline?\n\n7. The interaction narrative relies heavily on \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\" but this is a post-hoc explanation. Can you provide any predictive test -- e.g., predicting which pruning method will win for a new recovery paradigm (DPO, KTO) before running the experiment?\n\n## 7. Score\n\n**3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe pruning x recovery interaction is a novel and interesting research question. However, the evidence base is too thin for a top venue: single model, single GRPO run per condition (no multi-seed recovery), CKA is the weakest predictor in its own regression, the effect sizes are modest (5pp GRPO advantage, 8% incremental R^2, 12pp interaction from an under-powered 2x2), and important related work is missing. The paper would benefit from: (a) multi-seed GRPO runs to establish recovery variance, (b) validation on a second model, (c) comprehensive related work coverage, and (d) a stronger justification for CKA over simpler alternatives that outperform it in the regression analysis.\n\n## 8. Confidence\n\n**4/5 (High)**\n\nI am familiar with the structured pruning, CKA, and RLVR literatures. The experimental design concerns (single-seed recovery, ANOVA df) are well-grounded. I am less certain about the state-of-the-art in GRPO training stability, which could affect whether the 5pp gap is meaningful."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12467,"cache_read_input_tokens":61094,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12467,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XRXoNWibjzgLu2t7Vuw955","session_id":"e50da390-5c7e-4dcd-8358-bec3c8329c08","uuid":"668b1692-d3e1-4d05-9a2b-e53757d65e0c"}
